  "response": "Adjudication between outcome-based and duty-based accounts of rightness begins with a careful statement of what each side actually claims, since much of the apparent deadlock dissolves once the competing theses are formulated with precision. Consequentialism holds that the moral status of an act is fixed by the outcomes it produces, and that thesis cannot be squared with rule consequentialism, which assesses acts by their conformity to rules whose general acceptance maximizes good. By contrast, act utilitarianism evaluates an act solely by the value of the states of affairs it brings about; that is, on act utilitarianism nothing besides an act's outcomes bears on whether that act is right.\n\nWhereas the outcome-centred picture just sketched locates moral status entirely in what an act brings about, the rival tradition locates it in the character of the act-type itself. Deontology holds that certain act-types are forbidden regardless of the good they produce, a commitment that rules out threshold deontology, which allows a moral constraint to be breached once the stakes pass a specified threshold. By contrast with any licence keyed to the magnitude of the stakes, the most austere formulations of duty leave no room for such an escape. Kant's categorical imperative directs agents to act only on maxims they could will as universal laws. Ross, for his part, held that a prima facie duty can be overridden by a weightier competing duty. W. D. Ross held that the duty not to lie is absolute and never yields to any other duty, and an unyielding prohibition of that kind cannot both be maintained and permit a prima facie duty to be overridden by a weightier competing duty.\n\nThe decisive work in such disputes is done less by abstract argument than by the cases each theory must classify, and the literature on lying supplies the standard example. Kant's essay on lying was written as a reply to David Hume, and if that is the essay's occasion, it cannot have been prompted by the objection recorded next. Benjamin Constant pressed against Kant the case of lying to a murderer who is seeking his victim, which shows that philosophers test normative theories by comparing their verdicts with intuitions about particular cases.\n\nConsider the case in its concrete form, where even the description of what was done is contested. At least one of the following holds of the informant who shielded a hunted innocent, and perhaps both: the informant made a false statement to the officers about the fugitive's whereabouts, or the informant kept the fugitive's whereabouts from the officers. Which description is accurate matters, because a prohibition on asserting falsehoods and a prohibition on withholding what others are owed do not cover the same ground.\n\nNeither theory is simply refuted by a single recalcitrant verdict. What the case does is force each side to say precisely which feature of the situation carries the moral weight\u2014the outcome secured, the maxim adopted, or the description under which the act falls\u2014and then to accept the costs of that answer across the whole range of cases in which it must be applied. Adjudication proceeds, in the end, by weighing those costs against one another rather than by direct appeal to a principle taken to be self-evident.",
